Claims:
[
    "Drift is any kind of change that makes the world your model sees look different from the world it was trained and evaluated on.",
    "In the material we discussed, drift isn’t limited to just one thing—it can show up in several guises.",
    "The most common flavors are data drift, where the actual input data start to shift; model drift, where the model’s behavior itself changes over time; and concept drift, which is a mismatch between what users expect and what the model delivers.",
    "There’s even a kind of “user drift,” where the audience using the system changes – for example, a platform that was originally popular with millennials might suddenly see a wave of Gen‑Z users, and their expectations could be quite different.",
    "The offline evaluations you performed before launch are based on a snapshot of data and user expectations at that moment.",
    "If the data, the model, the underlying concept, or the user base shifts later on, the performance you measured in the lab no longer guarantees the same experience for real users.",
    "In the example from the notes, a model that was scoring well offline could start receiving thumbs‑downs because the new generation of users isn’t getting the answers they expect.",
    "That mismatch is precisely what drift captures.",
    "Drift matters because it can silently erode the quality of your AI system after it’s live.",
    "Even if you keep the input dynamics static—say you have a fixed set of 100 documents you query against—other forms of drift (like changing user expectations or subtle model aging) can still appear.",
    "Recognizing that drift is inevitable in any AI‑driven system helps you plan for ongoing monitoring and evaluation, rather than assuming that a one‑time offline test will hold forever."
] 

