Statements:
[
    "Sure, let’s break down what “computed” and “captured” signals mean when you’re doing an online evaluation of a chatbot.",
    "Think of a signal as any piece of information that tells you something about how the system is behaving or how the user is reacting.",
    "In an online setting you can get two kinds of signals.",
    "A captured signal is something that’s already there for you to record; you don’t have to do any extra work to derive it.",
    "It’s basically a raw observation that you can store directly.",
    "If a user clicks a thumbs‑up or a thumbs‑down button during the conversation, that click itself is a captured signal.",
    "You just log it in your tracking system (like LangSmith) exactly as it appears.",
    "Latency (the amount of time the chatbot took to produce an answer) is a captured signal.",
    "The number of tokens used is a captured signal.",
    "The monetary cost of a conversation is a captured signal.",
    "All of these are already measured by the system, so you simply capture and store them without any further calculation.",
    "A computed signal is something you have to derive yourself.",
    "These aren’t given to you automatically; you need to calculate or infer them from other data.",
    "The context doesn’t give a concrete example.",
    "You would take existing information—perhaps the raw logs, timestamps, or user actions—and apply some logic or formula to produce a new metric.",
    "Because you have to “figure out” or compute it, it’s called a computed signal.",
    "Captured signals are ready‑made observations like thumbs‑up/down, latency, token count, or cost that you just store.",
    "Computed signals are metrics you have to calculate yourself from the data you collect."
] 

